{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: The model consistently fails to capture the magnitude of price spikes (e.g., rising from ~70 to >80), often predicting only a flat or negligible increase. Apply a positive bias adjustment when the 18-day history shows a clear upward trend or when sentiment history contains non-zero values (0.20-0.33) indicating positive market signals.",
    "Implement aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model exhibits a 'lag' in recognizing the end of rallies, continuing to forecast gradual increases while true prices peak and then crash. When the forecast trajectory is upward but the recent history shows a sharp spike (e.g., >78), immediately adjust the forecast downward to anticipate a rapid correction.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon sensitivity: The model's 30-day forecast is too smooth and linear, ignoring the high volatility and cyclical nature of the data. Replace the linear extrapolation with a non-linear decay function that accelerates the decline after a peak and dampens the recovery after a trough.",
    "Weight sentiment history more heavily: Positive sentiment scores (0.20, 0.33) in the history correlate with actual price surges that the model misses entirely. Treat any non-zero sentiment value as a trigger to override the model's default flat/linear projection with a momentum-based spike forecast.",
    "Adjust for overshoot in the second half of the forecast window: The model consistently overestimates prices in the latter half of the 30-day window (e.g., predicting 69+ when actuals drop to 63). Apply a negative correction factor that increases in magnitude as the forecast horizon extends beyond 10 days."
  ]
}
